## 2. Sitemap (what changed and why)

Before: `/sitemap.xml` → 6 files (`/sitemap/0..5.xml`), 66,281 URLs total: 1,431 marketing + 64,853 mods in five 15,000-URL chunks. All under Google's limits (≤ 50,000 URLs, ≤ 50 MB uncompressed; the largest was 2.7 MB). Index `lastmod` identical for all children.

After: **one file per type** (`lib/sitemap-sections.ts`): `core`, `games`, `game-locations`, `locations`, `compare`, `tools`, `blog`, `servers`, `mods-1…N`. This makes Search Console's per-sitemap coverage report a per-template indexing report.

- `lastmod` only where a real change date exists (blog `updatedAt`, mod `upstreamUpdatedAt`/`updatedAt`, server `createdAt`). Templated pages have no per-page edit date; stamping the deploy time on 1,084 URLs taught crawlers to ignore the field site-wide. Absent is better than wrong.
- No `<priority>`/`<changefreq>` (ignored by Google).
- One entry per URL; slashless; redirecting URLs excluded (`lib/seo-redirects.ts`, kept in sync with `next.config.ts` by a unit test); `noindex` pages never listed.
- Mod chunks use keyset pagination (`id >= start`) instead of deep `OFFSET`.
- Policy (`lib/seo-index-policy.ts`, §4): game × location combos and mods are listed only where there is a reason to.

Old numeric sitemap URLs (`/sitemap/3.xml`) no longer exist. Only `/sitemap.xml` is referenced by robots.txt; if the owner submitted the numeric children in Search Console individually they should be replaced with the section files.

hreflang: **not needed** — one language, no regional variants of the same page. (The `<title>` currency fix in §1 removes the one place that behaved like a regional variant.) Adding hreflang to a single-language site is a common source of errors.

## 3. robots.txt, llms.txt, answer engines

- ChatGPT is the #1 acquisition channel (chat-referred entries in the last 30 days on non-bot marketing traffic: 374 on non-programmatic pages and 46 on game×location pages, versus 263 / 24 from Google). robots.txt now names the answer-engine agents explicitly (OAI-SearchBot, ChatGPT-User, PerplexityBot, Perplexity-User, Claude-SearchBot, Claude-User, DuckAssistBot, Applebot) so no future blanket
- `/llms.txt` now carries the facts an answer engine needs to cite us, all read live from the same tables checkout charges from: a full VPS spec/price table (CPU, RAM, NVMe, bandwidth, regular and introductory price), a table of every game we host with its from-price, RAM range and slot range, every free tool with its one-line description, and the 25 most recent guides. No fabricated figures.
- `/compare` now emits a real, server-rendered list of every comparison and migration page (visually hidden `<nav>`, a legitimate screen-reader list that mirrors the visible board).

## 4. Scaled-content exposure and what to prune (use real signals)

Google's spam policy on scaled content targets many pages made primarily to rank, with little unique value. Two families fit that profile in volume.

### Game × location (≈700 pages, `lib/marketing/gameLocationLandings.ts`)
- Similarity (5-word shingle Jaccard, full visible text): same game across five locations 0.81–0.87; same location across five games 0.36–0.55; VPS vs game page in one city 0.09–0.17.
- Demand: only **23 distinct** of these URLs received a human visit from a search engine or AI answer engine in the last 90 days (own `Visit` table, bot user agents excluded); in the last 30 days the human entries to every `*-server-hosting` page were dominated by the ~25 hand-built flagship game pages (`/minecraft-server-hosting` 74, `/valheim-server-hosting` 42, …). GSC "striking distance" (th
- Action shipped: the sitemap lists a combo only when its location has a real datacenter (Singapore, Germany, Canada) or the page has earned a search/AI-referred human visit in 90 days (`seo_gameloc_sitemap_mode`, default `demand`; `all` restores the old behaviour). Tail pages stay crawlable and indexable — nothing is removed from Google by this alone. A hard `noindex` on the tail is a one-line 
- Content upgrade shipped where it is real: the sibling links now rotate (see §5) so every combo is linked, and the location's general game page now links to every per-game page. No invented stats were added.

### Mods (64,853 pages, `/mods/<family>/<slug>`)
- Real data: 62,338 have ≥ 1,000 real upstream downloads, 27,491 have ≥ 10,000; 45,852 carry a long upstream description; 12,223 of 19,920 Minecraft mods have a one-line description under 80 characters. Unique value is the upstream facts plus one-click install; the framing text is templated.
- Demand: **28** distinct mod URLs received a search/AI-referred visit in 30 days. The 20,297 "entries" to mod pages in the same period have almost no referrer (20,224 direct) — these are overwhelmingly crawlers/unfiltered bots (bot filtering is agent A3's area), not demand.
- Action shipped (`seo_mod_sitemap_min_downloads`, default 10,000): only mods with ≥ 10,000 real downloads are listed in the sitemap (27,491 instead of 64,853); mods under `seo_mod_noindex_below_downloads` (default 100; 630 mods) get `noindex, follow`. Everything else stays crawlable and indexable. Set the first to 0 to list every mod again.
- These defaults are a judgment call made on the numbers above; they are Setting rows precisely so the owner can move them after reviewing GSC.

## 5. Internal linking (measured by `scripts/seo-link-graph.ts`, 1,680 pages crawled, all non-mod sitemap URLs + a mod sample)

| template | crawled | avg in-links | orphans | unreachable from home |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| game-location (`/<place>-<game>-server-hosting`) | 615 | 3.0 | 451 | 615 |
| location-landing (`/<place>-<service>`) | 160 | 6.8 | 0 | 160 |
| game-region (`/<game>-server-hosting/<region>`) | 141 | 3.0 | 0 | 0 |
